# Decide how to treat checks that do not apply: auth, A2A, WebMCP, DNS-AID, commerce

## Step

The [checks asset](./research-agent-ready-checks-asset.svx) settles the gate: Level 5 is Level 4 plus two of three groups, and this site honestly clears none of them as planned. The groups: (a) Web Bot Auth, a JWKS at `/.well-known/http-message-signatures-directory` whose private key signs the site's own outbound bot requests; (b) all four integrations, which adds an A2A agent card that is only truthful if `/a2a` implements A2A JSON-RPC; (c) auth metadata, OAuth discovery or an `auth.md` that names real registration endpoints.

Decide which two groups the site earns rather than fakes. Recommendation: (b) by implementing a minimal real A2A endpoint at `/a2a` whose one skill answers questions from the same knowledge base the MCP serves (A2A is JSON-RPC over HTTP, small enough for one Worker route), and (a) only if the site ever sends requests; otherwise (c) with an honest `auth.md` that says access is anonymous and documents no registration, if the scanner's parser accepts it (unverified; test against the live scanner before relying on it). If neither second group is honest, the finish line drops to "Level 4 plus every applicable check" and the brief says why. Commerce stays out of scope; WebMCP stays out because the root is not an HTML app.

## Result (2026-09-22)

Joel delegated the call. Level 5 is Level 4 plus two of three groups. Decision, in order of honesty:

1. Group (b), all four integrations: build a minimal real A2A endpoint at `/a2a` (JSON-RPC over HTTP, one skill that answers questions from the same `search` and `read` capabilities), so the agent card at `/.well-known/agent.json` describes a service that exists. Also publish the ARD manifest, which the v0.1 scan still lists and which is applicable.
2. Group (c), auth metadata: publish an honest `/auth.md` that states access is anonymous, there is no registration, and no OAuth server exists. Keep it only if the live scanner counts it; never publish OAuth discovery or protected-resource metadata for a server that does not exist.
3. If (c) does not count, group (a): a Web Bot Auth key directory at `/.well-known/http-message-signatures-directory` holding a real Ed25519 public key whose private half lives as a Worker secret, documented in `llms.txt` as the key rat-stack tooling signs with. This is the softest of the three and the brief says so; if Joel objects, the finish line becomes "Level 4 plus every applicable check".

Applicable and still missing after v0.1: DNS-AID (SVCB records under `_agents` in the Stack, DNSSEC on the zone) and the ARD manifest. Not applicable, stays out: WebMCP (no HTML app), every commerce check. All of this executes in [reach-level-five](./reach-level-five.svx).
